  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry and warm conditions expected through the end of\nthe week.\n\nHigh pressure approaching from the central CONUS will build\noverhead today and shift offshore through Saturday. This will\nyield well above average temperatures over the next several days\nalong with very dry air. Plenty of sunshine each day with \nhighs approaching the middle to upper 60s to near 70 by Friday \nacross the region. Lows at night will remain in the 30s to 40s \nthrough the week. One caveat to the prolonged dry conditions and\nwarming temperatures could have implications on fire weather (see\nFire Weather section below). \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Increasing confidence in a low pressure system \nimpacting our region Sunday into Monday.\n\nThis week's persistent upper-level ridge will gradually start to \nslide offshore late this week between Friday and Saturday, as a \nsecond ridge looks to continue building over the Northeast over the \nnext several days. This will result in continued dry conditions \nthrough the start of the weekend, with temperatures ranging well \nabove normal. \n\nAs we look towards late in the weekend into next week, model runs \nhave now been consistently showing a shortwave disturbance forming \nover the Southeast near the Florida peninsula that could begin to \ntraverse north late this weekend into early next week. Model runs \ncontinue to show large uncertainty in where exactly this low will \nend up, but several ensemble and ML outputs show increasing \nprecipitation chances across the Mid-Atlantic beginning late Sunday \n& into Monday. A large amount of this uncertainty is a result of a \npotent upper-level trough forming over the western half of CONUS and \ntracking east in the same time period, which will significantly \ninfluence where this southeastern low ends up. Model guidance on \nthis trough is still all over the place, so will continue to monitor \nas guidance gets a better handle on how these synoptic pieces will \ninteract. Regardless, it does seem that the potential is increasing \nfor cloudier, wetter weather overall early next week.",
